_margins":0.0,"_gate_fund_growth":null,"_gp_fund_growth":null,"_score_fund_growth":0.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.0,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":false,"_score_shrhldr_yield":11.1,"_gate_share_shrink":-0.15568020077960032,"_gp_share_shrink":true,"_score_share_shrink":100.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.09675290390707497,"_gp_sbc_dilution":false,"_score_sbc_dilution":0.0,"_gate_insider_own":0.00939,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":6.3,"_gate_insider_buying":null,"_gp_insider_buying":null,"_score_insider_buying":null,"pf":[],"description_full":"Wolfspeed, Inc., a semiconductor company, focuses on silicon carbide materials and devices for power applications in Europe, Hong Kong, Singapore, China, rest of Asia Pacific, Japan, the United States, and internationally. The company offers silicon carbide and gallium nitride (GaN) materials, including silicon carbide bare wafers, epitaxial wafers, and GaN epitaxial layers on silicon carbide wafers to manufacture products for RF, silicon carbide substrates, mosfets, schotky diodes, power, and other applications. It also provides power devices, such as silicon carbide Schottky diodes, metal oxide semiconductor field effect transistors (MOSFETs), and power modules for customers and distributors to use in various applications of automotive domains, including electric vehicles and fast charging, as well as existing and emerging applications in the industrial and energy domain, such as AI data centers, grid modernization, and renewable energy and storage. The company was formerly known as Cree, Inc. and changed its name to Wolfspeed, Inc. in October 2021. Wolfspeed, Inc. was founded in 1987 and is headquartered in Durham, North Carolina.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Robert A. Feurle | CEO & Director | Age 55 | Compensation: $1.6M (FY2026)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 2K people. Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (5/10).","financial_summary":["Wolfspeed, Inc. is a small-cap technology business with a $1.3B market cap.","The top line has compounded at +1% over the past three years and -7% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at -23% against a 8% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, with gross margins sitting at 30%.","The company is burning through $2.0B of cash, and without positive owner earnings any valuation rests on hope rather than evidence.","On the balance sheet, thin -4.2\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Piotroski score 3/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.7) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Very elevated short interest (54.2% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting against the business","sev":"red","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Value-trap profile (score 90/100): structural revenue/margin decline; leveraged balance sheet under pressure \u2014 cheapness may reflect deterioration, not mispricing","sev":"amber","cat":"value_trap"},{"text":"Financial quality metrics lag Technology peers","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Technology sector lagging the market significantly, underperforming by 7% over 3 months","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Elevated sector volatility (38% annualized) adds uncertainty","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Antitrust and platform-regulation risk \u2014 the larger the network effect, the larger the regulatory target on its back, and a single consent decree can reset the moat.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"AI disruption of existing software moats \u2014 incumbents with durable margins face commoditis